1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,181
Why do we sometimes use A, and sometimes THE?

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:06,650
For the answer, let’s visit England a thousand years ago!

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:11,157
In 1066, the king of England dies without an heir.

4
00:00:11,466 --> 00:00:13,470
Now England needs a king:

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:15,483
England needs a king.

6
00:00:16,133 --> 00:00:19,381
But three men each want the throne!

7
00:00:19,766 --> 00:00:21,599
First, Harold is crowned.

8
00:00:21,833 --> 00:00:23,800
But the king of Norway invades!

9
00:00:24,066 --> 00:00:25,067
Harold defeats him,

10
00:00:25,066 --> 00:00:26,967
but before he can catch his breath,

11
00:00:26,966 --> 00:00:29,399
William arrives with another army!

12
00:00:29,733 --> 00:00:32,458
This time, Harold is killed, and William becomes:

13
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:34,050
The king of England!

14
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:35,483
Notice what happened?

15
00:00:35,666 --> 00:00:37,416
First, we said A KING:

16
00:00:37,533 --> 00:00:38,672
a king—

17
00:00:38,766 --> 00:00:40,163
we haven’t specified which one.

18
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:43,798
But THE KING means that particular king!

19
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:47,729
The roots of this distinction go back to Old English!

20
00:00:48,066 --> 00:00:50,725
A comes from ĀN, meaning “one.”

21
00:00:51,033 --> 00:00:53,777
THE belongs to the demonstrative family, like “that”:

22
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,134
something like “that one” or “the very one.”

23
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:57,772
So what’s the key difference?

24
00:00:57,966 --> 00:01:01,633
When your listener can identify exactly which thing you mean,

25
00:01:01,633 --> 00:01:03,026
we usually use THE;

26
00:01:03,333 --> 00:01:05,197
even if it hasn’t been mentioned before!

27
00:01:05,433 --> 00:01:10,056
To practise, we’ve prepared a visual challenge and a practical worksheet.

28
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:12,452
Just comment THE and we’ll send them to you!
